tellraw @a {"text": "Greece (Syracuse, Macedon), Rome, gastraphetes c. 399 BCE; torsion engines from c. 340 BCE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The first artillery. The gastraphetes was a giant crossbow cocked by the belly; torsion", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "engines replaced the bow with two bundles of twisted sinew or hair that stored far more", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "energy, throwing bolts or stones of up to 80 kg.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Each arm is thrust through a skein of sinew rope wound tight in a frame; winding the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "bowstring back twists the skeins further. A trigger releases the arms. Philo of Byzantium", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "and Hero record a design formula: the spring-hole diameter in dactyls is 1.1 times the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "cube root of 100 times the shot weight in minas.", "color": "gray"}
